# NOTES
# - Group A (Jinqimen, Lianjiang, Linglong, Sanao) never returned results — its sub-agent process produced no output despite completing without error signal. Per coordinator instruction, all 28 fields for these 7 reactors are marked UNVERIFIABLE rather than left unresearched or guessed. These 4 plants/7 units need a follow-up verification pass with a fresh web-search budget.
# - Session-wide WebSearch quota (200 calls) was exhausted almost immediately across the four parallel agents, forcing all groups onto WebFetch against specific known URLs (Wikipedia, world-nuclear.org, power-technology.com) instead of iterative search-and-verify. This is why many LOW-confidence "single source" citations appear below — recommend a follow-up pass with a fresh WebSearch budget, run serially rather than in parallel, to raise confidence and fill UNVERIFIABLE gaps (especially Ningde 5&6, Sanmen 3&4, Qinshan turbine/RPV suppliers, and all of Group A).
# - CNPE/CNPEC in the original heuristic is likely a garbled/incorrect stand-in for two different real entities depending on plant: (a) SNERDI (Shanghai Nuclear Engineering Research & Design Institute) for CNNC's early indigenous designs (Qinshan 1, confirmed WRONG), and (b) CNPDC (China Nuclear Power Design Company) for CGN sites (Ningde, confirmed WRONG). Recommend a database-wide grep for "CNPEC"/"CNPE" as architect_engineer values and re-verifying each against plant ownership (CNNC vs CGN) rather than treating it as a single correct default.
# - CONFIRMED CNNC/CGN ownership mixup: Lufeng units 1 & 2 are listed with constructor=CNNC but Lufeng is a CGN site (Wikipedia + WNA both confirm "CGN Lufeng Nuclear Power Co." as operator, and Lufeng units 5 & 6 already correctly show CGN). This is exactly the confusion pattern this audit was designed to catch.
# - Sanmen 1 & 2 constructor/architect_engineer values appear swapped: sourcing describes SNPTC as general contractor and Westinghouse/Shaw Group as the engineering/AE-role provider, the reverse of the current DB assignment. Confidence MED (single Wikipedia-article synthesis) — recommend confirming against an NRC or Westinghouse project document before flipping.
# - Sanmen 2 rpv_manufacturer is very likely a copy-paste-from-Unit-1 error: Doosan made Unit 1's RPV but CFHI made Unit 2's RPV per an explicit, well-corroborated Wikipedia AP1000-article statement (China's first domestically produced AP1000 RPV).
# - Ningde rpv_manufacturer varies unit-by-unit (Unit1=Dongfang Electric, Unit2=Shanghai Electric, Units3&4=CFHI) — the blanket "CFHI for all" guess is only half-right, confirming the audit's per-plant-verification premise.
# - Ningde turbine_supplier for units 1-4 is sourced to Alstom (not Dongfang Electric as guessed) per a single power-technology.com citation ($135m contract for six 1,000MW turbine-generator packages); this is a MED-confidence but potentially high-impact correction and should be cross-checked against a second source before being treated as final.
# - Ling Ao 1&2 (Framatome-era M310+) constructor/architect_engineer values ("Framatome" / "EDF / CNPEC") both appear wrong: the project-company that actually built the plant was LANPC (a CGN joint venture), and the named architect-engineer in sourcing is Tractebel Engineering (Belgium), with EDF only in a consultancy role. Framatome's real role was as NSSS/reactor vendor, which is more consistent with the existing rpv_manufacturer field than with constructor.
# - CANDU units (Qinshan 3) structurally do not have a single "RPV" — they use a calandria + horizontal pressure-tube design. The existing "None" value is defensible as a schema/structural point, but the correct fabricator identity for the calandria/pressure tubes remains UNVERIFIABLE with sources checked this pass; consider whether the schema should carry a distinct note field for reactor-type-inapplicable cases rather than a bare "None."
# - Newest under-construction units at each site (Ningde 5&6, Sanmen 3&4, and all of Group A) have thin-to-nonexistent English-language secondary sourcing for turbine/RPV suppliers — this is a structural gap (recency), not a heuristic error, and should be flagged as "needs WNN construction-start article" in the backlog rather than re-guessed.
